# Nebula coding

## Do not extract single-use functions

If a block of code has one call site, leave it at that site. Do not invent a helper, lambda, or `static` function "for clarity." Extract only when a second caller exists, or the user asks for an API.

```cpp
// BAD — helper with a single caller
static uint64_t LookupFolderOrDefault(const Util::Dictionary<Util::String, uint64_t>& dict, const Util::String& path)
{
    IndexT i = dict.FindIndex(path);
    if (i == InvalidIndex)
        i = dict.FindIndex("proj:work/assets");
    return dict.ValueAtIndex(i);
}

// GOOD — that lookup stays at the call site until a second caller appears
```

## Data-oriented

Prefer plain structs, explicit arrays, integer ids, and transforms over data. Avoid new class hierarchies, inheritance, and virtual functions. If you think you need them, stop and argue why data, a function table, or an id + arrays cannot do it.

Do not use STL/STD containers, strings, or smart pointers (`std::vector`, `std::string`, `std::unordered_map`, `std::unique_ptr`, …). Use Nebula types: `Util::Array`, `Util::Dictionary`, `Util::String`, `Util::FixedArray`, `Ptr<>` where the engine already uses it, foundation allocators and id pools.

```cpp
// BAD
std::vector<Item> items;
std::string name;
if (ptr == nullptr) return;

// GOOD
Util::Array<Item> items;
Util::String name;
n_assert(ptr != nullptr);
```

## Fail fast — no defensive coding

Do not hide bugs. If an invariant is broken, crash. Do not clamp, skip, default, renormalize, or early-return to "make it work."

Invariants (null, OOB index, bad id, impossible state): `n_assert` / `n_assert2` / `n_assert_fmt`. Expected failure (file/network/user input): `n_error`, a log, or a handled error path that is visible. Recovery is never silent.

```cpp
// BAD — swallows the bug
if (ptr == nullptr) return;
if (index >= count) index = count - 1;
IndexT i = dict.FindIndex(path);
if (i == InvalidIndex)
    i = dict.FindIndex("fallback");

// GOOD
n_assert(ptr != nullptr);
n_assert(index < count);
IndexT i = dict.FindIndex(path);
n_assert(i != InvalidIndex);
```

Same in shaders: do not remap out-of-bounds work into valid data. No `ValidTexel` skips, no `min(lastTexel, coord)` clamps, no dummy 0 and continue. If the dispatch or indexing produces OOB, fix that. If OOB is inherent to the algorithm (e.g. SPD's 64×64 tiles), leave the coordinate alone — do not clamp it onto the last valid texel.
